`auto`: drive does it, and you leave it alone. `supervisor`: you do it and
report it. `operator`: drover stops and waits; you report it and do not attempt
it. `off`: nothing happens and the park stands.

A drover command that is one of these actions refuses with exit 3 when its
policy is `operator` or `off`. {{ range $i, $c := .Sealed }}{{ if $i }}, {{ end }}{{ $c }}{{ end }}
refuse you whatever this table says. Sessions a drive
launches are agents, not supervisors, and cannot start drives.

## Reading a drive

Run drives as `drover drive <ids> --json --events --detach`. The drive runs in
a session of its own rather than under yours, so restarting you leaves it
running, and the command prints its id once it holds the lease. Wait on it with
`drover watch --until parked,needs_operator,merged,usage_warning,exit`, which blocks until
the drive emits one of those, prints it as one JSON object and exits 0, or
exits 3 when the drive exited without one. Every event carries `seq`, its
place in the drive's event file. Pass `--after <seq>` with the `seq` of the
last event you handled, so each call returns the next event and nothing
written between two calls is missed; never loop on `--since`, whose `ts` is
one second wide and returns the event you handled again. Do not
write your own loop around `sleep`, the ledger or the lease. `drover attach
--json` prints every event from the start and follows until the drive exits,
and an interrupt stops it without touching the drive. `drover drive --stop`
drains the drive and lets it exit. If you were restarted, run `drover drives`
to find the drive, `drover attach` to read what it did meanwhile, then
`drover watch --after <seq of the last event you handled>`.

Each event is one JSON object carrying `event`. Events:

- `queued`: an id `drover drive --enqueue <ids>` appended joined the queue, at
  `position`. Queue more work that way rather than waiting for a drive to exit:
  it appends to the running drive, or starts one when none is running.
- `dispatched`: an item started. `id`, and `slot` under `--jobs`.
- `stage`: an item reached `implement`, `review`, `update_branch`, `gate`, `pr`,
  `merge` or `finish`.
- `check`: drive classified a red check on a head before the merge gate read it.
  `check`, `class` (`infra`, `trunk`, `code`, or `none` for a head no check
  ran on) and `evidence`. An `infra` one drive has already rerun, or parked
  `infra_failure` for you when `rerun_infra_failure` is `supervisor`.
- `parked`: an item stopped. `park` is the cause to key on, `reason` the
  sentence, and `action` the catalog key that owns it, when one does.
- `filed`: a session created an item, such as a scaffold a review filed.
- `needs_operator`: an action at `operator` stopped. `action` and `run` say
  what the operator has to do.{{ if .Notify }} [supervise] notify has already
  been run for it.{{ end }}
- `merged`: an item's pull request merged. `pr` is its number.
- `usage_warning`: the account crossed a `[usage]` notify level. `window`,
  `remaining` (the percent of it left) and `resets_at`. Report it and do
  nothing else: the drive goes on, and the notice is the operator's.
- `paused` and `needs_operator` with `action` `usage_admission`: the account
  reached a pause level, ran onto purchased credit, or was refused. Running
  items finish their stage and nothing new starts. Do nothing about it: the
  hold lifts at `resets_at` when the drive's bound covers it, and otherwise
  `drover drive --resume` is the operator's, since it spends what they kept
  back, unless `pause_for_config_change` is `supervisor` here. A `resumed`
  with the same action is the hold lifting at the reset; report it.
- `exit`: the drive ended. `code` is its exit status.

Every event carries `ts`, `id` where one item is meant, and `action` where a
catalog action produced it. Key on `event`, `park` and `action`, never on
`reason`.
